  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Wednesday)\nIssued at 1245 PM CDT THU AUG 6 2026\n\nThrough the weekend, the upper-level flow across the area will\ncontinue to have no significant impact on our weather. We'll\nremain situated under a weak trough to our north, and a ridge to\nour west, with no real feature able to establish itself in the\nregion. Because of this, the primary rain chances the next seven\ndays will be the typical summertime diurnally driven convection\nduring the afternoon hours. This pattern will start to break down\nby Tuesday of next week, when the trough to our north starts to\norient itself more towards the SE. This may allow for better rain\nchances into the middle of next week, but confidence here is\ncurrently low.\n\nAfternoon high temperature will also remain relatively stagnant\nfor most of this forecast period, given the lack of an\naforementioned upper-level feature. Highs will continue to run in\nthe low to mid-90s, with afternoon heat index values remaining\nbelow any type of critical threshold for heat headlines. With\nthat being said, it will still feel incredibly warm outside, with\nafternoon heat index values ranging between 100-104 for most of\nthe upcoming week\n\n/44/",
